  "intro": "# Everything else\n\nThe odds and ends: everything Black can play on move one that the earlier chapters do not cover. One safe recipe each, and none of them needs much memorising.\n\n## The Dutch, 1...f5\n\nThe one that changes something. Look at the b1 to h7 diagonal: the pawn on f5 sits right across it, so the bishop on d3 would be staring at its own dead end. The bishop goes to e2 instead.\n\nAfter that it is the King's Indian recipe. Black has no pawn on d5, so take the space with c4, castle early because Black's whole plan is a kingside attack, and meet ...Ne4 by challenging it with Nc3.\n\n## 1...c5, and the one exception to bishop first\n\nThis is the only line in the repertoire where e3 comes before Bf4, and it is worth knowing why rather than remembering it. Black has played ...c5 without ...d5, so the c7 square is already empty. That makes ...Qb6 stronger than usual, and it also means ...cxd4 comes without the loss of time that normally makes it harmless.\n\nSo play 2.e3, take back on d4 with the e-pawn, and bring the bishop out next move with a solid centre already behind it. One move later, and nothing lost.\n\n## 1...b6, 1...e6, 1...g6\n\nNothing changes. Bishop out, triangle, knight, castle. With ...b6 or ...e6 the bishop goes to d3 as usual, because the h7 diagonal is clear. With ...g6, wait one move: if Black follows with ...d6 you are in chapter 3, and if ...d5, chapter 4.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nPutting the bishop on d3 against the Dutch, where a pawn on f5 blocks it. Playing 2.Bf4 against 1...c5 out of habit and having to solve problems that e3 would have prevented. Reaching for a different opening, an early e4 or c4, because the position looks unfamiliar. Starting a kingside attack before castling, which the Dutch punishes harder than anything else.",
